	•	Section 5 Comments/Quotes: The target outcome was postoperative venous thrombosis, operationally assessed as lower-extremity DVT using serial colour Doppler ultrasonography. In the temporal validation set, the Random Forest model achieved AUC 0.895 (95% CI 0.788–1.000), accuracy 0.897, sensitivity 0.897, specificity 0.895, PPV 0.946, NPV 0.810, and F1-score 0.921. The abstract writes “PPV 0.946%,” but Table 3 reports 0.946, corresponding to 94.6%. Calibration curves were presented, and DCA indicated net clinical benefit over relevant threshold probabilities.

Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Inclusion required postoperative ECMO support and bilateral lower-limb Doppler examination before discharge, while patients with incomplete records, coagulation disorders, or severe ECMO contraindications were excluded. The study does not report consecutive recruitment or the number screened and excluded, creating substantial potential for selective inclusion in this small single-center cohort.
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Candidate predictors included postoperative laboratory results, respiratory failure, and anticoagulant administration. The model’s intended prediction time was not fixed, and anticoagulant therapy may be initiated because clinicians already perceive thrombosis risk or after evolving thrombotic findings, creating treatment-confounding and temporal-leakage risk.
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): Unclear | Quote/Reasoning: DVT ascertainment was rigorous, using bedside colour Doppler within 48 hours, repeat examinations when symptoms or D-dimer increased, and specialist confirmation. However, the paper labels the outcome as VTE while providing diagnostic criteria only for lower-extremity DVT and no explicit pulmonary-embolism definition, leaving the precise outcome construct unclear.
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The study included only 189 patients, and the RF AUC fell from 0.990 in training to 0.895 in validation, which the authors acknowledge as mild overfitting. It is not clearly stated that RFE was nested within training folds, the reported 70:30 split conflicts with the calendar-defined temporal cohorts, exact split counts are absent, and no geographically independent validation was performed. These limitations create substantial optimism and reproducibility concerns despite grid search, cross-validation, SMOTE, calibration, and DCA.
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: The study directly evaluates an interpretable machine-learning model for a clinically important postoperative complication in ECMO-supported lung transplant recipients and is directly applicable to AI research in thoracic organ transplantation.

